.TH PRINTF 3
.SH NAME
printf, fprintf, sprintf \- formatted output conversion
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <stdio.h>

int
printf(format [, arg ] ...)
char *format;

int
fprintf(stream, format [, arg ] ...)
FILE *stream;
char *format;

int
sprintf(s, format [, arg ] ...)
char *s, *format;
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B printf
writes on the standard output,
.B fprintf
on the named
.IR stream ,
and
.B sprintf
in the array
.IR s ,
followed by a NUL. Each converts, formats and writes its
.IR arg s
as the string
.I format
directs. The format holds plain characters, which are copied unchanged,
and conversion specifications, each of which converts the next
.IR arg .
.PP
A conversion specification is a % followed by, in order:
.TP 4
\(bu
Flags, none or several of:
.RS
.TP 8
.B \-
Put the converted value at the left of its field, with the padding
after it.
.TP
.B 0
Pad numbers with leading zeros instead of spaces. Ignored with
.B \-
and, for an integer, when a precision is given.
.TP
.B +
Begin a signed conversion with a sign, + or \-.
.TP
.I space
Begin a signed conversion that has no sign with a space.
+ wins over it.
.TP
.B #
The alternate form: for
.BR o ,
a leading 0; for
.B x
and
.BR X ,
a leading 0x or 0X before a value that is not 0; for
.BR e ,
.BR E ,
.BR f ,
.B g
and
.BR G ,
a decimal point always, and for g and G, the zeros at the end kept.
.RE
.TP 4
\(bu
A field width: the least number of characters the value takes. A
shorter value is padded with spaces (or zeros, with the flag 0) on the
left, or on the right with the flag \-. A longer one is not cut.
.TP
\(bu
A precision, written as a period and a number: for
.BR d ,
.BR i ,
.BR o ,
.BR u ,
.B x
and
.BR X ,
the least number of digits; for
.BR e ,
.B E
and
.BR f ,
the number of digits after the decimal point (6 if none is given); for
.B g
and
.BR G ,
the most significant digits; for
.BR s ,
the most characters printed from the string.
.TP
\(bu
.BR l ,
to say that the argument of d, i, o, u, x or X is a long.
.TP
\(bu
The conversion character:
.RS
.TP 8
.BR d ", " i
An int, in signed decimal.
.TP
.B u
An unsigned int, in decimal.
.TP
.B o
An unsigned int, in octal.
.TP
.BR x ", " X
An unsigned int, in hexadecimal, with the digits abcdef, or ABCDEF.
.TP
.B c
An int, printed as the character it is.
.TP
.B s
A string: a pointer to char; characters are printed up to the NUL, or
as many as the precision says. A null pointer prints as (null).
.TP
.B f
A double, in the style [\-]ddd.ddd.
.TP
.BR e ", " E
A double, in the style [\-]d.ddde\(+-dd, with at least two digits in
the exponent; E writes E.
.TP
.BR g ", " G
A double, in the style of f, or of e (E) when the exponent is less than
\-4 or not less than the precision; the zeros at the end of the
fraction, and a decimal point with no digits after it, are left off.
.TP
.B %
A %; no argument is used.
.RE
.PP
A width or precision may be given as *, which takes it from the next
argument, an int; a negative width means the flag \- and the width.
A float argument is passed as a double, so the same conversions serve
for both.
.PP
The floating point conversions print the digits of the double's exact
value, rounded correctly to the number asked for, however many that is.
An infinity prints as inf and a not-a-number as nan.
.SH "RETURN VALUES"
Each returns the number of characters written, not counting the NUL
that sprintf adds.
.B printf
and
.B fprintf
return EOF instead if the stream had a write error.
.SH EXAMPLES
To print a date and time, as "Sunday, July 3, 10:02":
.PP
.nf
	printf("%s, %s %d, %02d:%02d\en",
		weekday, month, day, hour, min);
.fi
.PP
To print pi to five decimal places:
.PP
.nf
	printf("pi = %.5f\en", 4 * atan(1.0));
.fi
.SH NOTES
The code for e, E, f, g and G is put into a program only when the
program needs it: cc includes it when the program passes a float or a
double to a function whose name ends in printf. A program that prints
doubles only through a function of its own, which hands them on to
printf, is then left without it, and stops with the message "printf:
floating point conversion not linked in". Naming the symbol __xfpcvt
includes it:
.PP
.nf
	extern int __xfpcvt;
	int *need_fp = &__xfpcvt;
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
putc(3), puts(3), scanf(3), ecvt(3), stdio(3)
.SH HISTORY
The
.B printf
function first appeared in Version 2 AT&T UNIX, and
.B fprintf
and
.B sprintf
in Version 7.
.SH BUGS
.B sprintf
cannot know the length of
.IR s ,
and a result too long for it overwrites whatever follows.
.PP
There is no h size; the l size is not used with c or s.
